1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Skip your period for months before age 40?

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,840
How fast you get diagnosed can shape your bone health for decades.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:14,800
This is called primary ovarian insufficiency, or POI - your ovaries stop working normally before 40 and estrogen drops.

4
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:22,640
Causes include ovarian surgery and cancer treatment, and autoimmune conditions - though for many women, the exact cause is never identified.

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:27,600
Surgery, chemotherapy, or radiation can damage ovarian tissue directly.

6
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:38,880
Separately, autoimmune conditions are estimated to cause anywhere from 4 to 30 percent of cases - the immune system mistakenly targets the ovaries, and follicle function drops off faster than expected.

7
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:43,760
Here's what most people miss: the timing of diagnosis changes bone outcomes.

8
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:55,360
In one study, women diagnosed within 6 months of their first symptoms had normal bone density 45 percent of the time, with osteoporosis in just 7.5 percent of cases.

9
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:00,320
Wait more than 3 years, and osteoporosis rates climb to 25 percent.

10
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:07,840
If your periods stop or turn irregular for 3 months or more before 40, get evaluated - a simple hormone blood test can confirm it.

11
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:13,760
Hormone therapy is typically recommended up to the average age of menopause to help support bone and heart health.

12
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:19,360
Pair it with enough calcium, vitamin D, and weight-bearing exercise like walking or strength training.
